######
#
# Build and Install ATS2 (ATS/Postiats)
#
######
#
# This is the one document of the build and installation of ATS2.
# README.md, CONTRIBUTING.md, src/README and AGENTS.md point here.
#
# Author: Hongwei Xi
# Authoremail: gmhwxi AT gmail DOT com
# Start time: 2013-09-02
#
# Last update: 2026-10-24 (ATS1 is not needed for anything; the bootstrap
# C bundle src/CBOOT is tracked)
#
######
#
# There are two ways to get ATS2:
#
#   A. the release tarball (for the users of ATS2)
#   B. the git repository (for the developers of ATS2)
#
# Both build with a C compiler and nothing else (no ATS compiler).
#
######

######
#
# Step 0: the requirements
#
######

* a C compiler (gcc or clang; the C99 and POSIX parts of libc)
* GNU make
* python3 (it generates the arena encodings of the syntax trees of the
  compiler; the tarball does not need it, a clone does)
* libgmp and its header (the package libgmp-dev on Debian and Ubuntu,
  gmp-devel on Fedora, gmp on Arch, `brew install gmp` on macOS)

libgmp is optional: without <gmp.h> the build switches by itself to the
integer backend of the constraint solver (it prints "KIND=intknd: no
<gmp.h> ..."), or ask for it:

  make KIND=intknd              # from a clone
  ./configure --without-gmp     # from the tarball

The integer backend is enough for almost every program; the gmp backend
solves constraints with integers that do not fit in a machine word.

######
#
# Step A: the release tarball
#
######

The releases are published as GitHub Releases:

  https://github.com/msorc/ATS-Postiats/releases

The tarball is ATS2-Postiats-x.y.z.tgz (with a .sha256 file next to it).
The tarball ATS2-Postiats-include-x.y.z.tgz holds the headers and CATS
files that are needed to use ATS2 from C. Say x.y.z is 0.4.3.

  sha256sum -c ATS2-Postiats-0.4.3.tgz.sha256
  tar -xzvf ATS2-Postiats-0.4.3.tgz
  cd ATS2-Postiats-0.4.3
  ./configure --prefix=/path/to/MYATS      # --without-gmp for no libgmp
  make
  make install

(the options of tar are `-xzvf`: the flag f is last, as it takes the
file name.) The tarball ships the bootstrap C bundle (src/CBOOT).

The install step puts ATS2 in

  /path/to/MYATS/lib/ats2-postiats-0.4.3     the PATSHOME of the install
  /path/to/MYATS/bin/{patsopt,patscc,myatscc,...}   scripts that set
                                             PATSHOME and run the programs
  /path/to/MYATS/share/man/man1/*.1          the man pages

so it is enough to have /path/to/MYATS/bin on the PATH:

  export PATH=/path/to/MYATS/bin:${PATH}
  patsopt --version

You need no other environment variable. The default prefix is /usr/local.

######
#
# Step B: the git repository
#
######

  git clone https://github.com/msorc/ATS-Postiats.git
  cd ATS-Postiats
  make

The first patsopt (the stage-0 compiler) is built from the C bundle
src/CBOOT, which is tracked in git; `make` does it by itself when there is
no bin/patsopt (the explicit form is `make bootstrap`). It then builds the
compiler with itself: bin/patsopt, bin/patscc, bin/myatscc, the library
src/libatsopt.a and the runtime library ccomp/atslib/lib/libatslib.a. The
build takes a few minutes; `make -j8` is fine.

The tree itself is the PATSHOME of the build:

  export PATSHOME=$(pwd)
  export PATH=${PATH}:${PATSHOME}/bin

To install it (the same layout as Step A):

  make install PREFIX=/path/to/MYATS

Other stage-0 compilers (an installed ATS2 release, an earlier build) are
a developer's option: see src/README.

######
#
# Hello, world
#
######

  printf '#include "share/atspre_staload.hats"\nimplement main0 () = println! ("Hello, world!")\n' > hello.dats
  patscc -o hello hello.dats
  ./hello

prints "Hello, world!". patscc needs no flag: it runs ${CC:-cc} with
-std=c99 -D_GNU_SOURCE (set PATSCCOMP to replace the whole C command).

######
#
# PREFIX, DESTDIR and uninstall
#
######

  make install PREFIX=/usr                     # default: /usr/local
  make install PREFIX=/usr DESTDIR=$PWD/stage  # for packagers: staged tree
  make uninstall PREFIX=/usr                   # removes what install made

DESTDIR is prepended to every installed path but it is not written in the
scripts of PREFIX/bin; PREFIX is. With the tarball, `make install` takes the
same variables (the prefix of ./configure is the default).

######
#
# The tools and the books
#
######

  make tools     # bin/atsdoc (the .atxt processor), bin/pats2xhtml
                 # (the syntax highlighter), libatsdoc.a, libatsynmark.a
  make book      # the HTML and the PDF of the books (doc/BOOK/README.md
                 # lists what they need: xsltproc, DocBook XSL, a browser)

`make install` installs the tools as well when they have been built.

######
#
# The tests
#
######

There is no unit-test framework: the tests are regression suites. They
need the tools (`make tools`).

  make check                             # typecheck every module of the compiler
  make test                              # all the suites below (several minutes)

or one by one:

  make -C src test                       # the compiler's own suites
  make -C utils/atsdoc test              # atsdoc and libatsdoc
  make -C utils/libatsynmark test        # libatsynmark and pats2xhtml
  make -C doc -f Makefile_test testall   # the examples and the code of the books

(put $PATSHOME/bin on the PATH first; `make test` does it). The md5 suites
and their conventions are described in AGENTS.md.

######
#
# The release tarballs
#
######

  make dist      # ATS2-Postiats-x.y.z.tgz, ATS2-Postiats-include-x.y.z.tgz
                 # and their .sha256 files, in doc/DISTRIB

It needs the tools (`make tools`) and the bootstrap bundle. The script
share/SCRIPT/build_release.sh does all of this for a given version; it
checks that the version of the release is declared consistently
(share/SCRIPT/check_release_version.sh). The process is described in
doc/DISTRIB/README and CONTRIBUTING.md.

######
#
# Cleaning
#
######

  make clean       # the build products of the compiler and the libraries
  make distclean   # everything: the tree is as after a clone

######
#
# Troubleshooting
#
######

* "gmp.h: No such file or directory" (while compiling patsopt, or a program
  that uses the gmp library): install the libgmp development package, or
  build without it: `make KIND=intknd` / `./configure --without-gmp`.
  Do not mix the two: after changing the kind, run `make distclean` first.

* "python3: not found" (or "python3: command not found") while building src/:
  install python3. A clone needs it to generate the arena encodings; the
  tarball needs no python.

* A patsopt/patscc of another installation is on the PATH (a distribution
  package of ATS2 0.4.2, an older install): the build uses the PATSOPT you
  give, else bin/patsopt of the tree, else the bundle, so it is not
  disturbed; but running `patscc` by name finds the first one on the PATH.
  Check with `which patscc patsopt` and `patsopt --version`; put
  $PATSHOME/bin (or PREFIX/bin) first. The compilers of 0.4.2 cannot read
  the prelude of this tree (it has no list.sats), and the other way round.

* "The environment variable PATSHOME is undefined!" when patsopt is run
  from bin/ directly: use the installed scripts (PREFIX/bin) or export
  PATSHOME as in Step B.

* "ATS-Postiats should have been available at '...'": the PATSHOME that the
  wrapper script holds does not exist (the install was moved, or it is a
  DESTDIR stage). Reinstall, or export the right PATSHOME.

* Strange compiler crashes at stage 1 with gcc 16 and an installed 0.4.2 as
  stage 0: see src/README ("-fno-strict-aliasing").

* Out of memory or very slow compilation of src/: use `make -j2`.

######
#
# Nix
#
######

  nix-shell nix/shell.nix   # a shell with ATS2 built from this tree
  nix build                 # the same, with the flake

###### end of [INSTALL] ######
